  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.4. Increased capacity of Member States to provide adequate labour protection to workers in diverse forms of work arrangements, including on digital labour platforms, and in informal employment</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. In November 2022 the domestic workers&#x2019; union, Jamaica Household Workers Union (JHWU) implemented a campaign to promote registration of domestic workers to the NIS, by information domestic workers and household employers about the benefits of registration. During the campaign, JHWU, in collaboration with the Ministry of Labour, disseminated 300 pamphlets, held a workshop for domestic workers on the steps of registration, and made a television appearance to raise public awareness. Jamaica Confederation of Trade Unions (JCTU) supported the awareness among their staff and members on the importance of NIS registration for domestic workers through holding 2 workshops in May and July 2022. 

2. JHWU organized and hosted computer literacy classes for its affiliates, providing them greater access to online banking, government services, and other e-learning services (https://our.today/jamaica-to-offer-domestic-worker-training-at-new-institute/). The union has also continued to mobilize support for the establishment of its own dedicated training center. 

3. The Ministry of Labour and Social Security initiated a grants programme targeting female domestic workers to foster formal entrepreneurship (May 2022). The grants aim to foster the participation of female domestic workers in formal entrepreneurial activities that generate additional income to supplement their livelihoods and ultimately, reduce their economic vulnerability. Beneficiaries of the programme were nominated by the Jamaica Household Workers&#x2019; Union. This initiative was developed in the framework of the Jamaica Formalization Project developed in 2020 with the expressed aim of expediting implementation of key interventions agreed in the National Transition to Formality Action Plan 2020-2022 (TFAP) and other urgent actions to mitigate the increased vulnerability of targeted workers induced by COVID-19. With contribution from RBSA, the ILO provided technical support to the content, design and printing of the brochure, and to the workshops. It also provided technical support in the provision of computing equipment. 

The ILO also supported the grants as a means of giving direct support to the MLSS to strengthen its entrepreneurship training and grant programme with resources specifically targeting female domestic workers.</narrative>
   </description>
